  step. Everything in that file is a global. The moment identity lives in its own file and "other forms" import it, that
  stops working cleanly: with plain script tags, load order becomes the dependency graph, and every module is a global
  anyone can overwrite. Note also that [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS Node files with no connection to the
  browser app — the repo already has two incompatible module conventions sitting next to each other.

  - [1mNative ES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m) — works in every current browser with no build step, real
    encapsulation, explicit dependencies. Costs: you must serve over [38;5;153mhttp://[39m rather than opening the file directly
    ([38;5;153mfile://[39m blocks module loading), and there's no bundling, so each module is a request.
  - [1mPlain script tags with a namespace global[0m ([38;5;153mwindow.Auth = {...}[39m) — zero change to how the page loads, works from
    [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Costs: manual load-order management, no encapsulation, and it gets worse with every file you add.
  - [1mA bundler[0m (Vite, esbuild) — modules plus dev server, HMR, and a production build. Costs: a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, a config,
    and a build step in a repo that currently has neither a dependency nor a script.

  [1mMy recommendation: native ES modules.[0m You've described at least three new units — an identity store, the tracking
  module, and the login flow itself — plus future forms consuming them. That's precisely the point where globals start
  causing bugs that are tedious to diagnose. ES modules get you there with no dependencies at all, which keeps this repo
  as simple as it is now. A bundler is a fine later step and switching to one from ES modules is easy; untangling
  globals into modules afterward is the expensive direction.

  I'd also fold the two stray CommonJS files in [38;5;153msrc/[39m into the decision — not rewrite them, just be explicit about
  whether the browser app and that Node code are meant to share anything.
